Watch</h2>
              <p>As we have already discussed coding standards are essential when working with a team, but how does this work in practice? Please see the following video to learn more.</p>

              <!-- sections.njk VIDEO! -->
              
              
              
      <div class="l-video-container">
        <video poster="~/images/svg/poster.svg" controls preload="metadata" aria-labelledby="watch" width="1000"
          data-base="cp3_u2_s1_p3_coding_standards_processes_tools" 
          data-ext=".mp4" 
          data-multi="false"
          data-trans="cp3_u2_s1_p3_coding_standards_processes_tools undefined">
          <source src="https://kpcontent.blob.core.windows.net/$web/resources/v2l/720/cp3_u2_s1_p3_coding_standards_processes_tools.mp4" type="video/mp4">
          <track default label="English" kind="captions" srclang="en" src="~/videos/captions/vtt/cp3_u2_s1_p3_coding_standards_processes_tools.vtt">
          <p>Sorry, your browser doesn't support embedded videos</p>
        </video>
      </div>
      
          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Leah:</strong> As we have already discussed coding standards are essential when working with a team, but how does this work in practice?</p>
<p>A developer team would initially meet to agree the rules that they would need to follow within their coding standards guide. This could consist of one that already exists and be used as a template or basis for creating their own coding style guide.</p>
<p>There are style guides and tools for programming languages, development environments, platforms, frameworks, and content management systems.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Style guides</p>
<p>A main consideration for choosing an existing style guide is the coding languages that are going to be used, as there are different standards that are written for different programming languages.</p>
<p>One of the most well used open sourced style guides for code standards is the Airbnb style guide. This includes style guides for JavaScript, ES5, React, CSS, Sass, and Ruby. It can be found on the GitHub platform and is one of the most popular style guides.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> There are other ‘style guides’, and these include:</p>
<p>• Codeguide</p>
<p>• Idiomatic</p>
<p>• Wikimedia</p>
<p>• Google</p>
<p>• WordPress</p>
<p>You can check out these websites on our weblinks section for more information.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Tools</p>
<p>As source code needs to be clean, consistent, and easy to maintain by existing developers, there are a number of tools that can be used in conjunction with the coding standards.</p>
<p>For example, StyleLint finds and fixes problems in code; the tool identifies and reports when the code is not consistent with the coding standards. It is easy to configure as the style guide is added as the default coding standard and the team can also add their own rules if needed.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Another example is a tool that formats the code such as Prettier. It is a library with a plugin that can be set to format your code each time you save your changes. It removes the original styling and makes sure that all the outputted code is a consistent style.</p>
<p>Other tools that are commonly used include:</p>
<p>• EditorConfig</p>
<p>• JSCS</p>
<p>• JSHint</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Official guides and standards</p>
<p>As well as open source style guides there are also official guides and standards that can be referenced. This can be useful for certain larger programming languages that are commonly used with proprietary software – some examples include:</p>
<p>• Microsoft .NET Framework</p>
<p>• Sun Systems</p>
<p>• Java Programming language</p>
<p>• C++ Programming language</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Code review</p>
<p>A code review which is also known as a peer review, is a form of software quality assurance. It is when one or more of the developer team would check each other’s source code in a given project.</p>
<p>Typical elements of a code review would be to include which standards would be used, first passes, using a ticketing system, run tests, check any changes made and then complete the review with an in-depth pass.</p>
<p>Code reviews would speed up the process of the project as this would ensure consistency and that the standards are being met across the whole team.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Static analysis</p>
<p>Static analysis also known as Static Application Security Testing [SAST], is a tool that is used to automatically check code for weaknesses that may cause issues later on. It finds potential quality issues in the code before the program is run.</p>
<p>It would be used to find and fix vulnerabilities, bugs, code errors, security risks, memory leaks, null pointers, buffer overflow, and any violations of the coding standard whilst the code is in a ‘static’ state [meaning whilst the code is not being run]. It is often used to comply with coding guidelines and industry standard.</p>
<p>Typical elements of static analysis would involve, writing code, running a static code analyser, reviewing results, fixing any issues and then testing.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Examples of static analysis tools include:</p>
<p>• SonarQube -</p>
<p>• Checkmarx SAST [CxSAST]</p>
<p>• Snyk Code, and</p>
<p>• Veracode Static Analysis.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
